@block introduction | start | - | Two by two determinants and invertibility
@prose Learn to calculate the determinant of a real 2x2 matrix, explain how swapping rows changes its sign, and use a zero or nonzero determinant to decide whether the matrix is invertible.
@endblock
@block definition | terms | 1.1 | Four positions and one determinant
@prose A two by two matrix A has entries a,b in its first row and c,d in its second. Its determinant is the real number ad-bc: multiply the main diagonal, multiply the other diagonal, and subtract in that order. The identity matrix I has ones on its main diagonal and zeros elsewhere. A vector v in the null space of A satisfies Av=0. A nonzero such vector witnesses singularity.
@display determinant_rule
A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}a&b\\c&d\end{array}\right],\qquad\det(A)=ad-bc
@endblock
@block proposition | rule | 1.2 | Nonzero determinant and inverse
@prose A is invertible when there is a matrix A^-1 with AA^-1=A^-1A=I; otherwise it is singular. When ad-bc is nonzero, the inverse is (1/(ad-bc))[d,-b;-c,a]. Multiplying A by that inverse must give the identity matrix [1,0;0,1], which is the direct inverse check used in this reading.
@endblock
@block proposition | condition | 1.3 | Zero, swaps, and what singular means
@prose Swapping two rows changes the determinant's sign. A zero determinant means the matrix is singular, not that a particular augmented system has a unique solution. If the first row [a,b] is nonzero and ad-bc=0, then (b,-a) is a nonzero null vector because its first row product is ab-ba=0 and its second is cb-da=0. If the first row is zero but the second row [c,d] is nonzero, use (d,-c): its second row product is cd-dc=0. For the all-zero matrix, every nonzero vector is a null vector. These cases are matrix facts, not a claim of a unique system outcome.
@endblock
@block example | worked | 1.4 | A separate inverse check
@prose Use a matrix that is not one of the question givens.
@display worked_given
A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}2&1\\5&3\end{array}\right]
@help hint
@prose Compute 2(3)-1(5). If it is nonzero, exchange the diagonal entries, negate the off-diagonal entries, and divide by the determinant.
@help answer
@display
A^{-1}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}3&-1\\-5&2\end{array}\right]
@help solution
@prose The determinant is 2(3)-1(5)=1, so A is invertible and no further scale is needed. Direct multiplication checks both rows and columns: [2,1;5,3][3,-1;-5,2]=[6-5,-2+2;15-15,-5+6]=[1,0;0,1].
@display inverse_check
AA^{-1}=\left[\begin{array}{cc}1&0\\0&1\end{array}\right]
@endblock
@block example | errors | 1.5 | Do not turn subtraction into addition
@prose A first line using ad+bc instead of ad-bc uses the wrong rule. If bc is nonzero, it also changes the numerical result; later arithmetic and an invertibility conclusion may inherit that error. Check the order of the two diagonal products before trusting a conclusion.
@endblock
@block exercise | practice | 1.6 | Three deliberate sets
@prose Teaching introduces the formula; practice introduces signed entries; fresh check includes a zero entry and dependent rows. Each set contains singular and nonsingular matrices. The invariant is the determinant formula and the zero/nonzero invertibility condition.
@endblock
@block summary | summary | 1.7 | Separate matrix facts from system claims
@prose Calculate ad-bc from the original entries. A nonzero result permits an inverse that must multiply back to identity. A zero result gives singularity and can be witnessed by a null vector. This family does not evaluate arbitrary-size determinants or infer a unique outcome for an unrelated augmented system.
@endblock
